# Who owns the paths inside the checkout, measured rather than inferred (2026-10-04)

`scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py` reads the container entry point and forbids the
shapes that write root-owned files into a developer's checkout. It is a static read of shell
text, and its own docstring says what it cannot see: a mount path assembled at runtime, a
`docker run` assembled inside a variable. `scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py` is the other
half. It walks the tree and asks, of every path, whether it belongs to the same owner as the
directory that contains it.

The two rules are not redundant, and the difference is not theoretical. Section 3 runs both
against the same tree with a leak in it: the static rule says the entry point is clean,
because the entry point *is* clean, and the runtime rule names the two paths the container
had just created.

Output below is quoted verbatim, including its own punctuation.

== 1. The comparison, and why it is against the root directory

The checkout arrives through a bind mount, so its root carries the uid of whoever cloned it,
while anything the container creates carries root. The obvious comparison -- "no path may be
owned by uid 0" -- is wrong on a machine with no POSIX ownership: on Windows every path
reports uid 0, the root included, so every path would be an intruder and the gate would fire
on every checkout there. Comparing against the owner of the root says "nothing here
disagrees with the directory containing it", which is the actual claim, needs no platform
branch, and is silent wherever one user legitimately owns everything.

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --verbose
      expected owner: chaos (1003)
      skipped directory names: target
      mount points known: 48
    tree-ownership: OK (13083 path(s) walked under chaos-code, all owned by chaos (1003), 1 path(s) skipped as pruned or mounted)

Thirteen thousand paths, `.git` included, in 0.165 seconds:

$ time python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py > /dev/null
    python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py > /dev/null  0.14s user 0.02s system 99% cpu 0.165 total

so the rule is cheap enough to be a normal gate rather than an opt-in diagnostic. For scale,
the same tree with `find`, both build output and `.git` pruned, is 8103 entries; the gate
walks those plus everything under `.git` and skips only the pruned name.

== 2. Three things are skipped rather than judged

* Symlinks are statted and never followed, so a link out of the tree cannot make this read
  files that are not in the checkout. A dangling link still has an owner and is judged like
  any path.
* A directory named by `--prune`, `target` by default, is skipped itself and with its
  contents. Build output was never part of the checkout, and inside the container the name
  is the mount point of the cargo volume, whose root really is root-owned; judging it there
  is a false positive. The leak that carve-out could hide -- the `target/` the runtime
  leaves in the checkout -- is what `check-container-hygiene.py` forbids statically, and it
  is visible from the host, where no volume is mounted over it.
* A mount point under the root is skipped with its subtree, read from
  `/proc/self/mountinfo`. What was deliberately mounted there came from somewhere else on
  purpose. A checkout that is itself a mount, which is what `/src` is inside the container,
  is still walked: the root is never one of the entries of its own listing.

The mount rule has a second job. `mountinfo` writes a space in a path as `\040`, so the
field is unescaped before it is compared;
`test_a_mount_point_whose_name_needs_an_escape_is_still_matched` pins that, and mutation M19
shows what happens when the unescaping is removed.

== 3. The leak, made on purpose, inside the container

A clone of `5e8ffb20` carrying the batch's four changed paths, mounted the way
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` mounts it, with the cargo volume over `/src/target`. The
chaos-owned `scripts/ci/__pycache__` that the clone inherited from the working tree is
removed first, so the directory the container makes is the container's own:

$ rm -rf /var/tmp/verify-own-clean/scripts/ci/__pycache__
$ find /var/tmp/verify-own-clean -user root
    (nothing listed)
$ docker run --rm --init --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src --volume chaos-verify-target:/src/target --env PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 chaos-verify:frozen bash -c 'mkdir -p /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__; touch /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc; mkdir -p /src/target/leak-under-the-volume; stat -c "%n owner=%u" /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__ /src/target; python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py; python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src'

Inside that container, in that order:

    -- who owns what the container just made
    /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__ owner=0
    /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc owner=0
    /src/target owner=0
    -- the static rule, which reads the entry point and finds nothing wrong with it
    container-hygiene: OK (16 shell script(s) scanned, 1 mounts the checkout, 1 Dockerfile(s), 0 problem(s))
    -- the runtime rule, which looks at the tree
    scripts/ci/__pycache__: owned by root (0), not by 1003, which owns src; it came from the other side of the mount, and the owner of the checkout cannot delete a directory the container created -- remove it with a privileged shell and stop whatever writes through the mount from doing it again
    scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc: owned by root (0), not by 1003, which owns src; it came from the other side of the mount, and the owner of the checkout cannot delete a directory the container created -- remove it with a privileged shell and stop whatever writes through the mount from doing it again
    tree-ownership: FAIL (2 path(s) not owned by 1003, 0 unreadable, 13016 path(s) walked under /src, 1 path(s) skipped)
    gate exit=1

`/src/target` is root-owned because it is the root of the named volume, and it is the one
path here the gate does not report: it is the pruned name. The directory the container made
under it is not in the walk at all, because the walk stops at the pruned name.

Note `not by 1003` rather than `not by chaos (1003)`: the image is Debian bookworm and has
no passwd entry for the host's uid, so `user_name()` falls back to the bare number. Both
spellings appear in this document, and they are the same user.

== 4. What the owner of the checkout can then do about it

$ find /var/tmp/verify-own-clean -user root
    root ./scripts/ci/__pycache__
    root ./scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc
$ cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean && rm -rf scripts/ci/__pycache__
    rm: cannot remove 'scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc': Permission denied
    rm exit=1
$ ls -ld /var/tmp/verify-own-clean/scripts/ci/__pycache__
    drwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Oct  4 14:07 ./scripts/ci/__pycache__

That is the whole reason the rule exists: the damage is not a stray file, it is a directory
the owner of the tree cannot remove. The same gate, run on the host against the same tree,
says the same thing with the names resolved:

$ cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean && python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py
    scripts/ci/__pycache__: owned by root (0), not by chaos (1003), which owns verify-own-clean; ...
    scripts/ci/__pycache__/check-doc-path-refs.cpython-311.pyc: owned by root (0), not by chaos (1003), which owns verify-own-clean; ...
    tree-ownership: FAIL (2 path(s) not owned by chaos (1003), 0 unreadable, 13016 path(s) walked under /var/tmp/verify-own-clean, 1 path(s) skipped)
    gate exit=1

The finding text is the same on both sides; only the way the expected owner is written
changes.

== 5. Removal, and the clean verdict

$ docker run --rm --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src chaos-verify:frozen rm -rf /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__ /src/target/leak-under-the-volume
    removal exit=0
$ find /var/tmp/verify-own-clean -user root
    (nothing listed)
$ cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean && python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py
    tree-ownership: OK (13014 path(s) walked under verify-own-clean, all owned by chaos (1003), 1 path(s) skipped as pruned or mounted)
    gate exit=0

== 6. The mounted-path rule, measured rather than argued

A tmpfs mounted inside the checkout, at a name nothing prunes. Docker mounts it mode 770
owned by root, which is exactly the shape the ownership rule reports:

$ docker run --rm --init --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src --tmpfs /var/tmp/verify-own-clean/docs chaos-verify:frozen bash -c 'stat -c "%n owner=%u mode=%a" /src/docs; python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src; python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src --mounts /proc/self/no-such-file'

    -- who owns the mounted directory
    /src/docs owner=0 mode=770
    -- the rule as shipped, which reads /proc/self/mountinfo
    tree-ownership: OK (12910 path(s) walked under src, all owned by 1003, 2 path(s) skipped as pruned or mounted)
    gate exit=0
    -- the same run with the mount list made unreadable, i.e. without the rule
    docs: owned by root (0), not by 1003, which owns src; it came from the other side of the mount, ...
    tree-ownership: FAIL (1 path(s) not owned by 1003, 0 unreadable, 12911 path(s) walked under /src, 1 path(s) skipped)
    gate exit=1
    -- and the count the mounted subtree takes out of the walk
      skipped directory names: target
      mount points known: 57
    tree-ownership: OK (12910 path(s) walked under src, all owned by 1003, 2 path(s) skipped as pruned or mounted)

So the rule is what makes the first verdict an OK rather than a false alarm, and the second
run proves it is the rule doing it and not the tree being clean. The walked counts differ by
one, not by the size of `docs/`, because a mount *hides* what is underneath: the 400-odd
tracked files under `docs/` were not in either walk. That the subtree of a mounted path is
not descended into is measured by the fixture instead, where a synthetic `mountinfo` naming
one directory takes the walk from nine judged paths down to three.

== 7. Both gates through the real entry point, at one tree state

The clone at the state the batch will be committed in -- `5e8ffb20` plus the two new scripts
and the two wiring edits -- so the two runs below describe the same fingerprint:

$ cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean && IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "tree ownership"
    == source tree: 4016 files, checksum 1392260633
       4 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
    == --only is in effect: 1 of 35 gates selected, this is not a sweep
    preflight: OK
    ...
    Ran 28 tests in 1.009s

    OK (skipped=1)
    tree-ownership: OK (13033 path(s) walked under src, all owned by 1003, 1 path(s) skipped as pruned or mounted)

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 35 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

$ cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean && IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "container hygiene"
    == source tree: 4016 files, checksum 1392260633
       4 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
    == --only is in effect: 1 of 35 gates selected, this is not a sweep
    preflight: OK
    Ran 19 tests in 0.595s

    OK
    container-hygiene: OK (16 shell script(s) scanned, 1 mounts the checkout, 1 Dockerfile(s), 0 problem(s))

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 35 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

Neither run leaves anything behind. After both, plus the leak and its removal above:

$ find /var/tmp/verify-own-clean -user root
    (nothing listed)

The clone does hold `scripts/__pycache__/check-doc-l10n.cpython-310.pyc`, timestamped Sep 23
and owned by chaos. It came across with the clone from the working tree, it predates
`PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, and being chaos-owned it is neither a finding nor a problem.
The ownership fixtures shell the gate out rather than importing it, so unlike the sixteen
`importlib` test modules they write no bytecode of their own.

== 8. What the fixtures cover, and where each one runs

Twenty-eight cases in `scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py`. Most of them cannot use
genuine ownership: creating a path owned by somebody else needs privileges, and on a host
without them a left-behind root-owned file is precisely the damage the gate reports -- it
would need root to clean up, on every run. So the per-path comparison is driven through
`--assume-owner-uid`, the seam the gate documents, and one class does it for real wherever
the process can undo what it creates:

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 28 tests in 1.074s

    OK (skipped=1)

On the host the skipped one is `test_a_directory_nobody_can_read_is_a_finding_not_a_silence`
-- root reads a `000` directory, so the branch cannot be reached there -- and
`RealOwnershipTests` is skipped as a class. Inside the container it is the other way round,
and the real-ownership case is the one that runs:

    test_only_the_path_another_owner_left_behind_is_flagged (__main__.RealOwnershipTests.test_only_the_path_another_owner_left_behind_is_flagged)
    One root-owned directory in a tree owned by someone else: exactly one finding. ... ok
    ...
    `chmod 000` is how a root-created directory looks to the owner of the tree. ... skipped 'root reads a 000 directory, so the branch cannot be reached here'

Both classes together mean neither environment is the only one holding up the rule: the
unreadable branch is proven on the host, genuine second-uid ownership in the container.

The container run earned its place immediately. The first version of that fixture passed on
the host, where the class is skipped, and failed the first time it actually ran:

    FAIL: test_only_the_path_another_owner_left_behind_is_flagged (__main__.RealOwnershipTests.test_only_the_path_another_owner_left_behind_is_flagged)
    One root-owned directory in a tree owned by someone else: exactly one finding.
    ------
    Traceback (most recent call last):
      File "/src/scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py", line 369, in test_only_the_path_another_owner_left_behind_is_flagged
        self.assertIn("1 path(s) not owned by 4242", proc.stderr)
    AssertionError: '1 path(s) not owned by 4242' not found in 'dangling: owned by root (0), not by 4242, ...
    link: owned by root (0), not by 4242, ...
    scripts/ci/__pycache__: owned by root (0), not by 4242, ...
    tree-ownership: FAIL (3 path(s) not owned by 4242, 0 unreadable, 11 path(s) walked under /tmp/tmpb6qw79si/repo, 1 path(s) skipped)

Three paths instead of one, because `os.chown` follows symlinks: it re-owns the target and
leaves the link itself, so the fixture's own two symlinks stayed root-owned. The gate was
right and the fixture was wrong; `follow_symlinks=False` in the fixture's setup is what the
failure bought.

== 9. The mutation matrix

`scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py` against twenty-two single-line mutations of the
gate. The driver refuses to report anything unless the baseline is green, treats an anchor
that does not match exactly once as survived, and restores the gate with a plain copy,
checked byte-for-byte afterwards:

    baseline green: Ran 28 tests in 1.121s
    killed   M1 owner comparison never matches: 12 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.054s)
    killed   M2 owner comparison always matches: 11 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.112s)
    killed   M3 --assume-owner-uid ignored in scan: 11 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.109s)
    killed   M4 expected owner in the verdict is a literal 0: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.102s)
    killed   M5 prune rule dead: 13 failing (Ran 28 tests in 14.265s)
    killed   M6 mount rule dead: 3 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.121s)
    killed   M7 symlinks followed when deciding to descend: 13 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.133s)
    killed   M8 ownership stat follows symlinks: 14 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.110s)
    killed   M9 no recursion into subdirectories: 14 failing (Ran 28 tests in 0.699s)
    killed   M10 only the first entry of each directory: 17 failing (Ran 28 tests in 0.727s)
    killed   M11 unreadable paths not counted: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.085s)
    killed   M12 skipped paths not counted: 5 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.087s)
    killed   M13 cap on printed findings removed: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.071s)
    killed   M14 the summary of hidden findings never printed: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.081s)
    killed   M15 an empty walk accepted as a pass: 2 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.092s)
    killed   M16 findings printed to stdout: 8 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.061s)
    killed   M17 unreadable alone does not fail the run: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.068s)
    killed   M18 the failing exit code is 0: 3 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.164s)
    killed   M19 mountinfo escapes left unescaped: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.141s)
    killed   M20 wrong mountinfo field taken as the mount point: 3 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.139s)
    killed   M21 default prune list emptied: 13 failing (Ran 28 tests in 14.282s)
    killed   M22 a file passed to --root is accepted: 1 failing (Ran 28 tests in 1.078s)
    == 22 mutations, 0 survived

M5 and M21 take fourteen seconds against the other mutations' one second, because with the
prune rule gone the walk descends into this machine's real `target/` directory. That is the
cost of the carve-out and the reason it is a default rather than a policy.

Two of the properties in the gate are guaranteed by the shape of the walk rather than by a
condition, and are said here instead of being counted as mutations: the root of the walk is
never one of the entries of its own listing, which is why a checkout that is itself a mount
is still walked, and the findings cap is applied to what is printed while `foreign` and
`unreadable` count every path. The fixtures for both
(`test_a_mount_point_at_the_root_does_not_empty_the_walk`,
`test_findings_are_capped_but_the_counts_are_not`) are contracts against a future rewrite of
the walk, not against a condition that exists today.

== 10. Wiring

The gate is in both roots, as `scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py` requires:

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (65 files in scripts/ci/, 64 reachable, 60 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --list | grep -n ownership
    19:tree ownership
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --only 'tree ownership'
    PASS  tree ownership

    all gates passed on the host (1 run, 0 skipped, 1 of 37 selected by --only)
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test | tail -1
    verify-gates self-test: 47/47 cases pass
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh | tail -1
    all gates passed on the host (33 run, 4 skipped)

The host runner counts 37 because it keeps the two build gates in its list and skips them;
the container counts 35 and runs all of them.

== 11. What this does not cover

* A path that is a mount point is not judged, so a mount point the runtime created *inside*
  the checkout is invisible while something is mounted on it. It becomes visible the moment
  the container exits, which is when it matters: the developer is the one who then cannot
  delete it. Section 3 of `docs/verification/container-hygiene-2026-10-04.log` measures that
  leak.
* Only ownership is measured, not permissions. A root-owned file that is world-writable is
  reported, and so is one that is not; a chaos-owned file with mode 000 is not.
* Group ownership is not compared. Two users sharing a checkout through a shared group would
  need that, and nothing in this repository's workflow does.
* The gate says "it came from the other side of the mount", which is the cause it exists
  for. A finding can also come from `sudo` on the host. Either way the remedy in the message
  is the same.
* `.git` is walked, on purpose: `git status` run as root inside the container refreshes the
  index, and an index that belongs to root is the same problem in a more surprising place.
  It was measured clean after the runs above.
* Nothing here stops the container from running as root. That is a deliberate property of
  the entry point, recorded in `docs/ci-test-debt.md`.

== 12. Reproduce it

The clean verdict, and the cost of the walk:

    python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --verbose

The three skip rules, each with its own fixture:

    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py -k prune
    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py -k mount
    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py -k symlink

The leak, in a clone so that nothing is risked in a real checkout. Any clone will do; the
paths the container creates are what is being measured:

    git clone --no-hardlinks . /var/tmp/verify-own-clean
    cd /var/tmp/verify-own-clean
    mkdir -p target
    docker run --rm --init --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src --volume chaos-verify-target:/src/target chaos-verify:frozen bash -c 'mkdir -p /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__ && touch /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__/probe.pyc && python3 scripts/ci/check-container-hygiene.py; python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src'

The first check exits 0 and the second exits 1 over the same tree. To take the leak away,
which needs the privileges that made it:

    docker run --rm --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src chaos-verify:frozen rm -rf /src/scripts/ci/__pycache__

The mounted-path rule, where the absence of a mount list is what makes the gate complain:

    docker run --rm --init --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/verify-own-clean:/src --tmpfs /var/tmp/verify-own-clean/docs chaos-verify:frozen bash -c 'python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src; python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py --root /src --mounts /proc/self/no-such-file'

Both gates through the entry point, and the ownership class that only a privileged run
executes:

    IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "tree ownership"
    IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "container hygiene"
